given the superhero genre is as known  for its eye-catching costumes as its  action it should come as no surprise  that birds of praise aesthetic is rooted  in the comics but how did the birds of  the screen compare to the birds of the  page clothes make a statement and the  statement shouted by Harley Quinn's  outfit is that more is most definitely  more necklaces heavy with charms acid  yellow overalls transparent plastic  motorcycle jackets heavy with fringe and  a galaxy's worth of patterns colors and  textures they're all in our closet and  vensim Harley never met a color she  didn't like piles on jewelry by the  pound and power clashes on principle if  there is a unifying factor to her  wardrobe it is her love of contrasting  the traditionally feminine against the  rugged pigtails red lipstick and  ostentatious jewelry sit alongside  ragged cut-offs stick and poke tattoos  and smeared eyeshadow the Harley Quinn  of cartoons and comics however sports a  very different look her debut in the  batman the animated series episode  Joker's favor established what was to be  the characters dominant design for years  to come a sleek unitard in red and black  it's pattern echoing traditional jesters  costumes that costume endures to this  day though recent DC Comics have  expanded her wardrobe fairly  dramatically if a dominant look has  emerged  it's the roller derby inspired design  created by artist Amanda Connor for the  characters 2014 solo series even that  outfit retains the red and black of the  1992 design however marking the  cinematic Harley as having a truly  unique look  birds of praise Black Canary sports a  look as intimidating as it is cool  onstage she is swathed in black fabric  her hair framing her face with a  dramatic curtain of bronze elsewhere she  dons a gold crop top high-waisted pants  and a plethora of accessories she's a  little bit Punk a little bit glam and a  little bit goth and in combining them  all with a toss of her hair entirely her  own brand of cool the Black Canary of  the comics is among the best-dressed of  the DC Universe as well yet in very  different fashion the characters most  well-known look features a black leotard  jacket fishnet tights and boots it's a  costume that can lean more or less moto  chic raid punk or pristine bombshell  depending on the situation though it's  starkly different from her movie duds  its spirit is similar striking stylish  versatile fashion for a woman as  comfortable in the streets as she is on  the stage  black mask isn't into subtlety he is  after all Lord of his club bound Kingdom  why not shake up the typical business  man's wardrobe his suits might be as  well cut as any bankers but he has them  made in green velvet and floral prints  paired with monogrammed gloves and vivid  pocket squares there's a flamboyant to  the villain rooted in his success he  dresses according to his own taste  because he's built a world in which his  tastes are the only ones that matter the  black mask of the comics in contrast  sticks to the classics his suits are  beautifully made but largely understated  if he branches out it's by wearing  pinstripes a double-breasted cut or a  simple pocket square this understated  canvas highlights the true departure of  his design the black mask he takes his  name from carved from the ebony wood of  his father's coffin he is rarely seen  without it he's a terrifying man who  lives to see his enemies their cronies  and more than a few innocent bystanders  suffer beneath his thumb not the kind of  dude to experiment with sunglasses but  very much the sort to enjoy trading his  normal face in for a terrifyingly  inhuman facade birds of praise Victor  Zsasz looks like the consummate creep  his clothes aren't much to look at but  the way he wears them says it all he  slouches smears and never seems to be  without a knife hidden in one of his  many pockets all the better to brandish  at the birds add in a bleached crop of  hair and prominent facial scars and he  looks every inch the violent enforcer of  black masks sinister intentions that he  is the Victor Zsasz of the comics is  similarly unpleasant but two very  different ends he's painfully thin often  shirtless and absolutely covered in  tally marks one for each life he's  claimed this Zsasz is a demented serial  killer and his negligible clothing  underlines this fact his eyes tend to  bulge and roll  all his teeth to clinch and manic Glee a  knife ever-present in his wiry hands the  cinematics as might look like the jerk  lurking in the alley but the comic zest  looks like he's straight out of a  nightmare the Cassandra Cain of the  birds of prey movies sports a look  typical of any skater kid you knew  growing up clothes for running hanging  out lying low her cut-offs are the  classic kind having clearly begun their  life as actual jeans she took scissors  too instead of something created in the  factory topped by layers of hoodies  t-shirts and jackets her most  distinguishing feature is the hot pink  cast on her arm crowded with doodles of  guns playing cards and other icons  she's the punk kid in every sense  deliberately ragged uninterested in  fashion and likely to be kicked out of  any given corner store for loitering do  I look like I'm worth half a million  dollars not really the comics Cassandra  Cain sports a far sleeker look each  superheroic persona she's claimed  Batgirl black bat and orphan being the  three most prominent has come clad in  black and yellow mostly fit to her body  for maximum stealth her Batgirl costume  likely still her most well known look  obscures her hair and face exchanges the  classic yellow gloves and boots for  black ones and hollows out the bat  symbol on her chest into a simple yellow  outline she's a shadow of the Gotham  skyline devastating and unstoppable in  her delivery of justice played by the  incomparable Rosie Perez birds of praise  Renee Montoya is a pillar of power our  clothes enforce this sleek slacks  button-downs and blazers all say that  this is not a woman to be messed with  her clothes are pared down to the  essentials there are no pleats at the  case or patterns to be found on Renee  Montoya the steely police officer only  offsets her look with a simple gold  chain and occasionally a pair of brass  knuckles  generally speaking the Renee of the  comics keeps things similarly simple but  there's a casual edge to her clothes  that the cinematic Renee lacks  especially when she's on the job though  Renee does wear more formal clothes now  and again she favors t-shirts jeans and  leather jackets as the question a role  renee assumed in the 2006 52 series  she keeps things a little more formal  wearing a suit tie fedora and face  obscuring mask in both universes Renee's  clothes make one thing crystal clear she  is absolutely not to be messed with  and in her practical shoes and bootcut  slacks absolutely able to chase you down  if you make an ill-advised break for it  unlike certain other members of the  birds of prey  Huntress largely sacrifices fashion for  function sure she enjoys shades of  purple crop tops and leather but she  dispenses with the piled on jewelry  prints and whimsical jackets of her  friends her look is tactical even  militaristic incorporating body armor  motorcycle gear and the movies only  utility belt with their trademark  crossbow in hand she cuts a fearsome  figure indeed which is exactly what the  ex mafia princess wants huntress  maintains a similar look in the comics  favoring purple leather and enough  pouches to keep her well stocked in  weaponry but there are also a few  theatrical details the cinematic  Huntress might fall ket most prominent  is her mask a purple affair that at its  most dramatic rises to two sharp points  above her hairline then there are the  cross motifs she's often incorporated a  whole host of crop tops and belly  cutouts quite a bit more revealing than  our movie counterparts and closets worth  